---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import GettingStartedSafety from '../../../components/GettingStartedSafety.astro';
import { exercisesInGroup, loadGuide } from '../../../lib/guide';

const data = await loadGuide('getting-started');
---
<Base section="getting-started" title="Progression" description="How to find your starting level, move up, hold or step back, and retest, for the eight-week bodyweight plan.">
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Getting started', href: '/getting-started/' }, { label: 'Progression' }]} />
  <section class="hero page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Progression</p>
    <h1>Finding your level and moving up</h1>
    <p class="lede">Instead of adding weight, you move up a ladder. The rules below are cautious planning conventions for beginners, not results from a trial.</p>
  </section>

  <GettingStartedSafety />

  <section aria-labelledby="placement">
    <h2 id="placement">Placement test</h2>
    <p class="prose">Do each test once, on a day you feel normal, after a warm-up. Start on the first level where you can do the test cleanly. If you cannot do the easiest level without pain, skip that pattern and ask a clinician.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Placement tests" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Pattern</th><th scope="col">Test</th><th scope="col">Ladder, easier to harder</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.groups.map(group => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><a href={`/getting-started/exercises/#group-${group.id}`}>{group.data.title}</a></th>
              <td>{group.data.test}</td>
              <td>{exercisesInGroup(data, group.id).map(exercise => exercise.data.name).join(', then ')}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="rules">
    <h2 id="rules">When to move up, hold or step back</h2>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li><b>Move up one level</b> when you reach the top of the rep range on every set with the reps in reserve for that week, and your technique stayed clean on the last rep.</li>
      <li><b>Add a rep, not a level,</b> while you are still inside the range.</li>
      <li><b>Hold</b> your level if technique slips, you are unusually sore the next day, or you slept badly.</li>
      <li><b>Step back one level</b> after illness, a break of more than a couple of weeks, or any pain. If pain does not settle within 2 to 3 weeks, see a clinician.</li>
      <li><b>Soreness.</b> Muscle soreness in the first week or two is common and eases. Sharp or joint pain is different: use the pain rule.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="retest">
    <h2 id="retest">After week 8</h2>
    <p class="prose">Retest each ladder. If you moved up, repeat the eight weeks at the new level. If not, repeat the plan. When the top rung of a ladder feels easy, you are ready for the next step. That is beyond this plan, so consider a coach, or the <a href="/strength/">strength section</a> if you have access to a gym.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
